   (Un-eagle-eyed readers, note: the 'm' is lowercase in the second line,
which will produce a compiler diagnostic if NERF_HEALING_MAXHP_POINTS is
not defined. Strangely, that was supposed to be the line that was 
/un/modified between old and new revisions; if you use conditional
compilation this way, you should /never/ change the behavior of the
"unpatched" code path.)

>      #endif
>
> will take you hours to debug if you have an old
> compiler that doesn't warn you about undeclared
> variable usages.

   No such compilers exist.  (At least, none that will compile any of the
Nethack code, which is written in C. Such compilers do exist for, say,
Perl.)
   The single-letter typo "#ifndef" for "#ifdef" is orders of magnitude
more likely, and no C compiler will diagnose that error for you; you'll
have to rely on reading your own code.  Another common typo is
"NERF_HEALING_MAXHP_BOOST" for "NERF_HEALING_MAXHP_BOOSTS"; again,
no C compiler will diagnose that error.
   (Therefore, it ought to make sense that your code should be as readable
as possible. But, as I say, human readability doesn't have to be the
/main/ reason to prefer line-orientation. The main reason is to make
things easier for your user: the 'patch' utility.)

> There's a reason skilled coders put only and exactly
(heh!)
> the stuff that changes with the value of the conditioning
> variable inside the conditional part of the compilation,
> and it is one of the software development "motherhood
> issues": say stuff _once_.
>
> It doesn't matter a bit to the compiler that the
> resulting code "reads ugly".

   It matters to the patch utility, though, which is important. And
perhaps even more important to a player who enjoys patching the game,
it matters to human readers.

   No two ways about it --- line-oriented patches are better.
Arguments from Perl will not avail you.

